WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.250
In a turn of events that surprised exactly
no one, Star Wars: The Force Awakens became

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.870
one of the biggest hits of the past several
years, and scored some pretty great reviews

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:09.870
to boot.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.450
It borrowed heavily from the first Star Wars
film, sure, but it sprinkled in enough fresh

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.460
ideas to balance out the nostalgia perfectly.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.920
So the film is pretty great!

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.770
But...that doesn't mean there aren't a few
things in there that might make you scratch

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:22.770
your head.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.550
Here are the dumb things we all ignored in
Star Wars: The Force Awakens.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.939
So...what's the First Order's whole deal anyway?

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:34.170
In the original Star Wars trilogy, the Rebellion
fights for freedom from the tyranny of the

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.289
Empire.

00:00:35.289 --> 00:00:40.790
The Empire is, well, an empire: it's a huge,
evil government that rules the galaxy with

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:41.840
an iron fist.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.170
Makes sense, right?

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.809
But in The Force Awakens, it's the Resistance
that fights against the First Order.

00:00:47.809 --> 00:00:51.570
And the First Order is...what, exactly?

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.660
If the Rebellion defeated the Empire, and
the galaxy is now governed by the New Republic,

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.750
then what the heck is the First Order?

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.660
What do they do, and why?

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.420
And where'd they get the resources to build
an entire planet made of Death Stars?

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.310
Curious fans can hit up Google for the First
Order's unexplained backstory.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:12.670
But seriously: we shouldn't have to chase
down the answers to some pretty basic questions

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:18.259
by digging into The Force Awakens Visual Dictionary
that kids buy at elementary school book fairs.

00:01:18.259 --> 00:01:20.340
R2-D2's perfect timing

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.119
That little data file that led to Luke's hidden
location served as a great carrot to dangle

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:30.439
in front of everyone and keep this movie,
uh, rolling.

00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:34.800
If there's one thing Star Wars does great,
it's hiding important information in adorable

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.800
robots.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:36.800
"What message?

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.700
The one that you've just been playing.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.180
The one that you're carrying in your rusty
innards."

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.369
But BB-8 only has a piece of the map.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:48.439
So, naturally, the little guy wonders about
other droid who spent some time as a rolling

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:50.719
flash drive: R2-D2.

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:55.259
In fact, he asks R2 about whether or not he's
got the rest of the map everybody needs.

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:56.920
And then this happens:

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.710
"You're wasting your time.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:04.119
It is very doubtful that R2 would have the
rest of the map in his backup data.

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:05.439
I'm afraid not.

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:09.750
R2 has been in low power mode ever since Master
Luke went away."

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:14.600
But C-3PO is totally wrong, as we find out
at the end of the film, when R2 suddenly wakes

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.140
up — just in time to put everybody back
into a good mood.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.820
That's great timing, R2.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.610
But...hadn't the Resistance ever tried to
look through R2's memory before?

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.300
He literally has a computer for a brain!

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.490
Nobody dug out a USB cable and plugged him
into the computer?

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.490
No one in the Resistance has heard of Ctrl-F?

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.140
Captain Phasma is not very Phantastic

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:41.470
Captain Phasma is undeniably cool, what with
her shiny armor, fancy cape, and slick British

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:42.470
accent.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.560
And then...she goes down like a sack of potatoes.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.900
And then, she pretty much caves immediately.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.810
Finn tells her to bring down the shields across
the base, and she does it without a fight.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:57.010
Did she only get that fancy stormtrooper suit
because she's, like, General Hux's cousin

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.200
or something?

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.950
Hunks don't need parachutes

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:04.650
Remember when Finn and Poe crash land on Jakku,
and Finn takes Poe's jacket from the smoking

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.810
wreckage?

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:10.900
It sure seemed like Poe was dead...until he
showed up again for the last act of the movie.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.510
We never find out how he survived.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.620
Or even why he left his styling jacket behind.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.620
The reason?

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:22.120
Poe was actually dead — at least, in the
first bunch of drafts of the screenplay.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.450
Apparently director J.J.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:27.840
Abrams was so taken with actor Oscar Isaac's
performance, he decided to keep Poe around

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.840
after all.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.640
Too bad we can't say the same for every pilot
in Star Wars, of course.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.880
"Cover me, Porkins."

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:34.980
"I'm right with you Red 3."

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.360
"Got a problem here."

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.360
"Eject."

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.700
"I can hold it."

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.030
"Pull up."

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.030
"No, I'm alright…"

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.370
Sorry, Porkins.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.390
Shoulda been handsomer.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:48.140
A Most Convenient Chasm

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:53.280
The film ends with a climactic battle between
Rey and Kylo Ren, as she takes the emo Vader

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.140
wannabe to the woodshed.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.760
By the end of the fight, Kylo Ren has a few
holes in him and Rey is triumphant.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:04.610
Then, a very convenient hole opens up right
between them, allowing Ren to be rescued before

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:05.660
Starkiller Base explodes.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:11.450
This, of course, is highly symbolic of the
ideological divide between Rey and Kylo Ren.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:15.340
One has embarked on the path of the Jedi,
while the other has turned his back on it

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.340
forever.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:17.480
Good versus evil.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:18.710
Dark versus light.

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:22.850
Oh, and keeping your exciting new bad guy
alive so he can come back and give your heroes

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:24.570
a hard time in the sequel.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:33.950
Too bad we can't say the same for every exciting
new bad guy in Star Wars, of course.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:39.740
Sorry, Darth Maul.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:42.680
Shoulda been, uh...Kylo Rennier.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:43.850
Thanks for watching!

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.440
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.630
Plus check out this other cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

